DIESEL RUN CALCULATOR

Diesel Run Calculator — a real-world task using only today's concepts: returning several values from one function (and unpacking them), def, parameters, return, calling functions, comparisons, if / else, while validation, variables, input(), float(), arithmetic, and printing with 2-decimal money format. No imports, no tricks.

Solve it individually, then come together as a group to compare approaches. Good luck!

A haulage company in Trans Amadi runs two trucks. After every trip the dispatcher works out three figures per truck: what the diesel cost, how far each litre pushed the truck, and what each kilometre cost. One function must work out all three and RETURN them together.

The function takes distance in km, litres used, and price per litre, and returns three values: total cost (litres x price), consumption (km per litre), cost per km (total cost / distance).

The program must:
- Ask for truck A's trip (distance, litres, price per litre - decimals allowed), call the function ONCE, unpack the three returns, and print:
  TRUCK A - COST: N52000.00 | KM PER LITRE: 2.50 | PER KM: N520.00
- Then the same for truck B.
- End by comparing the two cost-per-km figures and printing one line: TRUCK B ran cheaper per km - with the cheaper truck's letter.
- If the two work out exactly equal, print: BOTH trucks matched per km

Sample run:

Truck A distance (km): 100
Truck A litres: 40
Price per litre: 1300
TRUCK A - COST: N52000.00 | KM PER LITRE: 2.50 | PER KM: N520.00
Truck B distance (km): 150
Truck B litres: 45
Price per litre: 1300
TRUCK B - COST: N58500.00 | KM PER LITRE: 3.33 | PER KM: N390.00
TRUCK B ran cheaper per km

Edge cases to handle: a trip where both trucks cost the same per km (the matched line), litres that don't divide the distance evenly (2.5km per litre and similar - your two-decimal format does the rounding for display), a very short trip of 1km, and the test of the day: ONE call per truck - if you call the function three times to get three values, you missed the point; return them together and unpack.
